A||||30||Raydar||Building a new 'puter. Questions.||||5||
Z||000000||Raydar||10-23-2000||11:33 PM||||Okay, most of the stuff is on order.<BR>Abit BP6, Two 1st gen Celerons @ 600 Mhz, 256 MB of PC133, Probably gonna go with a 20-30 GB Quantum ATA HD. Have Win2K ready to load. <BR>Anybody familiar with this Motherboard? I've heard some "gotchas", especially w/ BIOS, and <I>especially</I> with Win2K.<BR>Is there any first-hand experience out there?<BR>Also, I'm looking for advice for a good general quality AGP video board. Not gonna run any killer games to speak of. <BR>"Why the dual CPU?" you are asking. <BR>Planning on burning CDs, and it will also become a network server if and when I can ever get broadband. Overkill, now. Hopefully not, later.  <BR>TIA! <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||5
Z||000001||frontal lobe||10-24-2000||12:20 AM||||I put together a computer and am using a Diamond Viper v550 with 16mg ram.  Works fine.  You could probably pick one up now for $35 to 40.  Viper v770 for $60 to 70.  Geforce for about $100.<P>If you don't mind using used parts, go to <A HREF="http://www.anandtech.com," TARGET=_blank>www.anandtech.com,</A>  go to forums, and go to "for sale/trade".  Usually lots of good condition parts available for good prices.  If you must have new, go to <A HREF="http://www.pricewatch.com." TARGET=_blank>www.pricewatch.com.</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||10-24-2000||09:25 AM||||Thanks for the info, Doctor!<BR>Pricewatch.com is where I found my Celeries. $87 each. Great site!<BR>I'll have to check the other.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-24-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000003||LarryB||10-24-2000||12:29 PM||||Raydar, if you haven't done so yet go to <A HREF="http://www.bp6.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.bp6.com</A>  for answers, anything I can spew they can explain better.   I have 2 BP6, 1 running NT 4.0 WS, the other Solaris x86.  Works fine for both of them but I only use the ATA-66 controller on the NT machine.<P>You need to check your processors too, Celeron 533A (vs. Cel 533) and faster Celerons are all 2nd generation and don't support SMP.  Celeron 533 is the fastest 1st gen Celeron. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||10-24-2000||12:58 PM||||Larry, Thanks for the info.<BR>The Celerons were listed on Pricewatch as "PPGA 600" (not FCPGA). There were two different places selling them. Are you referring to a 2nd gen that is still PPGA form factor? I was under the impression that "2nd gen" meant "FCPGA". Coulda been a typo, too. I'll make sure they are (or aren't) correct, before I power 'em up. Talked to several folks and have gotten several different opinions. The worst that will happen is I'll have to send 'em back and use 533s.<BR>I will check out BP6.com, too. Thanks!<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000005||LarryB||10-24-2000||02:48 PM||||PPGA is technically correct only for 1st gen cpus and Celeron II are truly FC-PGA.  If you got retail CPUS it will be clear on the box, if you got OEM look through the case. The Celeron-II is green and the processor casing is smaller than the fingernail on your little ffinger, there is also a sticker on the top with something like 600/66/128K on it.<P>1st gen Celeron is black with silver for the pins and the processor casing is silvery metal larger than your thumbnail.<P>Physically check before plugging and powering up the CPUs.  FC-PGA processors don't like being powered up in a PPGA socket :-O<P>Good luck! The worst you'll end up with isw] with dual 1st gen 533's, and that will make for a snappy machine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||10-24-2000||04:22 PM||||Larry, I believe that what I'm gonna end up with is Celeron IIs.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>I've been prowling around the net, and it looks like folks are calling these things PPGA when they are actually FCPGA. <BR>Thanks for the heads-up. I probably woulda generated some smoke.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>533's are just fine, though. Just saw the 600's and jumped.<BR>As one of my former managers said...<BR>"There's absolutely no substitute for knowing what you're doing."<BR>This is becoming a real learning experience.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||LarryB||10-24-2000||09:31 PM||||You're very welcome. Thanks for the opportunity to help in a small way. <P>-- Larry||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||10-24-2000||11:14 PM||||Raydar dude, the 566 MHz Celeron II is the best bargain going. Most of them are stable when overclocked at 100 MHz bus speed making them run at 850 MHz. I have one in my Abit SE6 that's been running at 850 all summer, no problem. I even got it to run at 950, but switched back to 850.<P>Doc, with all due respect, the Diamond Viper cards are obsolete has beens. Diamond is not supporting their products worth a crap since last winter when they lost key contracts and nearly went belly up. I have a Viper 550 16 meg card right now that I'm upgrading to a 32 meg Geforce 2 MX (probably the Creative Labs version). These are awesome budget 3D video cards that run about $150 or less. I wanted to get the 64 meg Geforce 2 GTS card, but those are about $400. Another good bargain for about $100 or less is the 3DFX Voodoo 3 card.||||reg||1
Z||000009||frontal lobe||10-24-2000||11:58 PM||||Batboy, no problem.  I'm not in love with diamond products, just that there were a lot of them out there with nvidia's chips.  These tnt chips are older now so pretty cheap.  But if you don't do much 3d gaming, I think they work fine.  There are plenty of other companies that used tnt chipsets.  Do you find them decent?<P>There are some geforce (mx?) lower end geforce cards that are still supposed to be decent performers that runs about $100.<P>Raydar, you should give us a price range on how much you want to spend on a video card.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||10-25-2000||01:11 AM||||You should be able to find the first generation Geforce cards pretty cheap, now that the geforce 2 is out.  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||10-25-2000||07:34 AM||||Doc, actually my Viper 550 did well over the last few years, however, I was helping it along in the 3D gaming aspect with a Voodoo 2 accelerator card. Diamond used to be a great company. Maybe it will be again. They suck right now. Can't get any support out of them. I was trying to get my Diamond 3D sound card working in my new motherboard. Everything worked, including the sound card, except the 3D part to my second set of speakers. Obviously needed an updated driver. The tech support first told me I had to reformat my harddrive??? I told him I was not losing 12 gig of data and that I'd done this before and that everything was working except the 3D sound. He said "oh". After looking up stuff and suggesting lame things, I asked to speak to his boss. After a lengthy wait, the same tech came back and told me his boss said that sound card won't not work with my new setup and that I should buy a different motherboard. I told them it was much easier to buy a non-Diamond card that worked. I asked when the last time the drivers had been updated. The last time was last winter when the dropped that model out of production. I hung up on them and proceeded to do two evenings of tweaking on my own and got it to work finally.<P>Standard is right! I forgot about the Geforce 1 cards, they would be a real bargain now too. Those TNT cards were great in their day, but they really are "was beens" now.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||10-25-2000||11:31 PM||||<B>Batboy, Doc, Standard...</B><BR>Thanks for the input. Haven't really made up my mind about the video card, yet. Probably $100-150 range, but that is <I>not</I> cast in stone. I don't have any requirements, in terms of games. Might want to watch a bit of TV, though.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <BR>Anything that I find is going to be an improvement over what I'm accustomed to. <BR>(Diamond Stealth 2500, S3, 2MB. and ELSA Victory 3D, S3, 2 MB. Both, state of the stone-age art.)<P>As for the 566 Celeron IIs, It's the same situation that I almost charged into with the 600s. (<B>Thanks, Larry B.</B>) They're FCPGA. The BP6 doesn't support it. Just PPGA. There are some adapters available, but apparently nobody has been able to get them to run SMP (dual) on the BP6.<BR>Just gonna have to use the 533s. Once I've determined that I've got a working system, then I'll overclock 'em. (Might even be able to take the space heater out of the office.)     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Thanks again to all for your input. <BR>These things don't work nearly as well once you let the smoke out.<BR>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||frontal lobe||10-25-2000||11:57 PM||||Excellent.  If you are going to put that kind of money into a video card (I don't blame you) then definitely a geforce based card will be more than great!<P>What are you going to do now, sell the fcpga's and buy ppga's?  Or is their another mother board you could use that would support dual fcpga's?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Raydar||10-26-2000||12:29 AM||||I (ahem) spoke with the folks who sold me the FCPGAs. Told them that they were likely <I>not</I> PPGA 600s, as described on Pricewatch, because they weren't supposed to even exist. He said to send 'em back! (Actually haven't received them, yet.)<BR>My cynical nature doesn't quite expect it to be that easy, though. If it works out, I'll pass the vendor's name along. If not, I probably will, too. Although I have to accept *some* of the blame, for not knowing what I was doing. I did suggest they should modify their ad.<BR>As for another Mobo, Abit has just released the VP6. Haven't seen it for sale yet, but I expect it's gonna be <I>expensive</I>. <BR>Got a good price on the BP6, though. Should keep me happy for several years.  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000015||frontal lobe||10-26-2000||12:04 PM||||Good luck.  Let us know how it turns out.||||reg||1
Z||000016||batboy||10-26-2000||09:11 PM||||I know nothing about the Abit BP6, but I had an Abit BH6 (I know, single CPU rather than a dual) that I upgraded from a Celeron 300A to a Celeron 566. I did have to flash the bios with an undated one from the Abit website. Then I used an Abit converter card and everything worked perfect. I suppose it couldn't be that easy on your setup?<P>A word of warning. There are two Celeron 533 chips. The old style 533 and the newer flip chip "coppermine" style, sometimes referred to as the 533A.<P>Doc, you kill me. Thinking $100 to $150 is a lot for a good video card. I've been toying with buying a GeForce 2 GTS 64 meg card (Hercules or Creative Labs), but decided $400 was just a little too expensive. Those $150 MX cards are looking like a bargain right now...LOL!||||reg||1
Z||000017||frontal lobe||10-27-2000||12:55 AM||||Batboy, I kill myself!  Even though I'm doing pretty well (no car payments, only 6 yrs to go on the house) I just can't make myself splurge.  $400 for a video card!!!  That's usually my goal on how much to put a SYSTEM together for!  That's part of the fun for me--see how decent a system I can put together for how little money.  But I'm not 3d gaming.  So now all I have to do is see if I can find a geforce mx somewhere for under $100...||||reg||1
Z||000018||frontal lobe||10-27-2000||02:31 AM||||Raydar and Batboy,<P>Check out the review of Creative Labs Annihilator2 ultra geforce card on <A HREF="http://www.anandtech.com." TARGET=_blank>www.anandtech.com.</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Raydar||10-29-2000||12:22 PM||||Okay... Received the 600s. They are the "FC" variety. They're going back if I can't sell them locally, today.<BR>Called the Mobo vendor, yesterday. Found out that it still hadn't even shipped. Asked him to cancel.<BR>Found the BP6 ($10 cheaper) and the 533 PPGA processors, about 30 minutes from here. Picked 'em up yesterday, along with a 32MB GeForce2 AGP card. Got the case and memory earlier in the week. <BR>(That sucking sound is getting really loud.)<BR>Have an extra 2GB Quantum HDD from one of my other experiments, that I'm gonna use to get the thing operational. Gonna shop around for a larger one, -20-30 GB. <BR>NIC, modem (56K USR Faxmodem), and sound card (Creative Labs with Yamaha SG wavetable card)will be pirated from the old one. <BR>Should be fun.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>...and I still can't get DSL or cable broadband.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Once again, thanks to all for sharing their experience / opinions / guidance. <P><BR>------------------<BR>Raydar<P><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||LarryB||10-30-2000||04:22 PM||||If you're going to buy an IDE hard drive get an IBM 75GXP 7200RPM drive, they have both 20 and 30GB versions.  Tough to find a better drive, street price for each is about $140 and $165 respectively.<P>Too bad about no cable/DSL availability.  I've had cable modem for 3+ years and it would break my heart to have to go to 56K dialup. <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||batboy||10-30-2000||08:44 PM||||Hey Raydar, if you still are looking for a harddrive, check out the Maxtor 40 gig 7200 rpm drives like mine. This is the second Maxtor I've had and both are in my machine right now. I love 'em. IBM and Seagate both are making some great drives right now too.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Raydar||10-31-2000||12:42 AM||||It's Alive!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Just got finished loading the OS. Still have to copy all my stuff from the old 'puter. <BR>Geez this is fun! <BR>Thanks for all the suggestions for Hard drives. I'm gonna have to do something, quickly. W2K takes just over 1 Gig of space on a FAT partition. <BR>Thanks again to all who shared their advice and experience. This was *thoroughly* painless.<P>Raydar||||reg||10
Z||000023||frontal lobe||10-31-2000||01:44 PM||||In the immortal words of Hannibal Smith, "I love it when a plan comes together."  Trivia question--what's that an OBSCURE reference to?<P>Assembling a computer is kind of like putting together a three dimensional, functional puzzle.  I'm glad it all came together for you.<P>Now, what's the next upgrade going to be...||||reg||1
Z||000024||Raydar||10-31-2000||03:18 PM||||<B>"I love it when a plan comes together."</B> <BR>Not too sure about the obscure reference. I use a variation of this statement, frequently. <P>Still have to come up with another hard drive. Will add a CDRW at some point. (Have an ancient Sony CD-R, now.) Once I make sure everything is stable, I'll probably see how fast I can overclock it without it melting down. Will need some better coolers, too. <BR>BTW... W2K is a quantum leap ahead of NT 4.<BR>From the time I booted to the setup disk, to the time I posted my message on the Forum last night, the machine only had to reboot 3-4 times. Unlike NT, which requires a reboot pretty much every time you blink.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>"If I have to fake 'genuine concern' one more time today, I'm gonna scream!" -TCH<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-31-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||frontal lobe||10-31-2000||06:19 PM||||Lots of nice, inexpensive hard drive options out there now.  I have a maxtor 20gig, 7200rpm.  No problems.  IBM, quantum, and seagate probably all have similar models for around $100.  How much space does someone need now, even WITH microsoft hogging 1 GIG!!??<P>Clue on the trivia:  "I pity the fool that can't come up with the answer."||||reg||1
Z||000026||LarryB||10-31-2000||09:45 PM||||Raydar, you need to choose carefully heat sinks for the BP6, mostly because there is so little room around the CPU sockets.  If you're using an Inwin mid-tower design you need to be careful as well.<P>One that will work is at <A HREF="http://www.3dfxcool.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.3dfxcool.com</A>  "LilMoFo" or something like that, I believe its a rebadged GlobalWin heat sink. <P>If you are going to attempt overclocking you should be wary of using Maxtor drives. They have earned a reputation of having problems when the PCI bus (and therefore the IDE controllers) is overclocked.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000027||batboy||11-01-2000||12:45 AM||||Shhh! Don't tell my 13 gig Maxtor that, it's been running overclocked for a year and a half.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Raydar||11-01-2000||09:17 AM||||This is the case that I have... <A HREF="http://www.macase.com/KA-580.HTM" TARGET=_blank>http://www.macase.com/KA-580.HTM</A> <BR>Cooling was the first consideration. Also,  there is almost enough room to climb in with my toolbox to work on stuff.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <BR>I'm using generic ball-bearing coolers, now. (Actually look like the THO fans on the 3DFX page. Maybe a little taller.) CPUs run in the 105-115 range, with no thermal compound (temporarily) between the chips and the coolers. When I overclock, I'll use Peltier coolers, if I need to. There is also plenty of room to add more case fans. There is already one in the front and one in the PS. <BR>I'm leaning toward a Quantum Fireball HD. Quantum makes the quietest IDE drive I've ever heard (or not heard). <BR>In the past, there have been some issues with Maxtor drives not booting correctly with the BP6. Not sure if those have been resolved or not. <P>Raydar <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 11-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||LarryB||11-01-2000||11:15 AM||||In a BP6? <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by batboy:<BR><B>Shhh! Don't tell my 13 gig Maxtor that, it's been running overclocked for a year and a half.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000030||batboy||11-01-2000||10:40 PM||||Good point, I have not used that mobo combo.<P>Raydar, you can't go wrong with those Quantum drives. I've got a couple buddies that have the Fireballs and they love 'em.||||reg||1
